==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Latinized translation of {{etyl|fr|en}} {{m|fr|Bohème}}, from {{etyl|LL.|en}} {{m|la|Boiohaemum}}, compound of Germanic {{m|gem-pro|*haimaz||home}} (more at {{l|en|home}}) and ''[[w:Boii|Boio]]''- ‘the Boii’, the Celtic tribe previously inhabiting the area. Bohemia was abandoned by the Boii ''ca''. 60 BCE. and settled by the Germanic [[w:Marcomanni|Marcomanni]] shortly thereafter.<ref>Dietz, Karlheinz (Würzburg). “Boiohaemum.” ''Brill’s New Pauly'', 2012. Reference. 14 March 2012 [http://referenceworks.brillonline.com/entries/brill-s-new-pauly/boiohaemum-e218860]</ref> Related to {{l|en|Bavaria}}.

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# a [[region]] in the west of the former [[Czechoslovakia]] and present-day [[Czech Republic]].

